{"author":{"id":"5698b6d16d951325374257d86a2e673da0e55970c3d21c0f7c6f49018f24e5bf"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1786281536,"nonce":"P0yBhG6d95LQEGr0X7vtrPR9qeE=","title":"**What is wrong.** `b268f52` added a bound rejecting a NUL byte in a label value, with","message":"`errs` code `invalid_label_value`. Postgres `text` cannot store U+0000 at all, so such a\nvalue fails at INSERT no matter how it is serialised — the bound is correct.\n\nBut `invalid_label_value` has no member in the `ingest_rejections.reason` enum, so\n`internal/ingestion/domain.ReasonFromError` maps it to `undecodable`. That is honest but\nlossy: the payload decoded perfectly well, and oto knows exactly which label failed and\nwhy.\n\n**Why it matters.** `ingest_rejections` is the only place a rejected alert survives —\nCONTEXT.md §5b's whole reason for rejecting per-alert and still answering 202 is that\nthe rejection is recorded rather than lost. An operator debugging \"why did oto drop this\nalert\" reads that reason column. `undecodable` sends them to look for malformed JSON,\nwhich they will not find, for a payload that was fine apart from one control character\nin one label value.\n\n**What it needs.** More than a constant, which is why it was not done inline:\n\n- `internal/ingestion/domain/rejection.go` — the constant and `reasonSet`\n- a migration re-adding `ingest_rejections_reason_ck` with the new member (expand /\n  contract: the widened CHECK must land before anything writes the new value)\n- `docs/design/SPEC.md` §L.3.2 and the DDL listing around line 807\n\n**Related, and probably the same change.** Two more storability gaps of the same class\nare open, both accepted by the domain today and both fatal at layer 6:\n\n- **Invalid UTF-8.** Postgres `text` in a UTF8 database rejects invalid byte sequences,\n  so `{\"a\": \"\\xff\"}` passes `NewLabels` and fails at INSERT. The adversarial corpus in\n  `internal/alerts/domain/labels_test.go` deliberately includes `\\xff` as *a value the\n  domain accepts*, so that test will flip the moment this is fixed.\n- **Annotation values.** Same unconstrained charset, stored in JSONB, which also cannot\n  hold ``. Deliberately left alone in `b268f52` because annotation policy at\n  ingest is truncate-and-keep rather than reject, so this is a product decision and not\n  a bounds check.\n\n**Done when.** A label value that cannot be stored is rejected with a reason that names\nwhat was wrong, that reason is a first-class `ingest_rejections.reason` member, and the\nUTF-8 and annotation cases are either fixed the same way or explicitly ruled out in the\nSPEC.","files":null}]}